<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bernardo de Monteagudo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1789">1789</a>–<a href="/years/1825">1825</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Monteagudo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bernardo de Monteagudo was a revolutionary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-american-wars-of-independence">Spanish American wars of independence</a>, born in Tucumán. He served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-de-san-martin">José de San Martín</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> and then in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, where as minister of war and foreign affairs in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">Protectorate</a> he dominated government. His harsh measures against Spaniards made him hated, and he was deposed and exiled in 1822. He was murdered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> in 1825.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bernardo-de-monteagudo.jpg" alt="Bernardo de Monteagudo" width="320" height="424" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: V.S. Noroña · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bernardo_de_Monteagudo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Monteagudo was the son of Miguel Monteagudo, a Spaniard, and Catalina Cáceres Bramajo of Tucumán, and the only survivor of eleven children. He was most likely of mixed race, with African slaves among his ancestors; some historians hold that his mother had been the slave of a canon, and his political enemies later used the colonial statutes of blood purity against him. He studied law in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> and at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sucre">Chuquisaca</a>, where he graduated in 1808 and began to practise as a defender of the poor.</p><p>That year, when news came of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, he wrote the Dialogue between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atahualpa">Atahualpa</a> and Fernando VII, which argued that since the King was prevented from ruling, the Indies must rule themselves.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> from 1811 he defended Castelli in the trial over the defeat of Huaqui, edited La Gazeta de Buenos-Ayres and influenced the drafting of the Provisional Statute. In 1817, a few days after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-chacabuco">Battle of Chacabuco</a>, he crossed the Andes to serve <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-de-san-martin">José de San Martín</a> as auditor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/army-of-the-andes">Army of the Andes</a>. He wrote the Proclamation of the Independence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> in January 1818, though Miguel de Zañartu's claim to it is disputed, and advised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernardo-ohiggins">Bernardo O'Higgins</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-maipu">Battle of Maipú</a> he was involved in the summary execution of the brothers Juan José and Luis Carrera, and probably in the murder of Manuel Rodríguez Erdoíza.</p><p>In exile after 1822 he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama">Panama</a> and met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">Simón Bolívar</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibarra">Ibarra</a> in 1823, then travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala-city">Guatemala City</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-cecilio-del-valle">José Cecilio del Valle</a> shared his idea of a continental congress. Defying the ban on his return, he came back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> as a colonel under Bolívar and entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ayacucho">Battle of Ayacucho</a> on 9 December 1824. By then he believed that all Hispanic America should be a single nation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>At around 8 p.m. on 28 January 1825, Monteagudo was walking from his home in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> to the house of Juana Salguero when he was stabbed to death, at the age of thirty-five. He is remembered as one of the revolutionaries who served in the independence struggles of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-la-plata">Río de la Plata</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, and as a key figure in the history of people of African descent in the region in the 18th and 19th centuries.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bernardo de Monteagudo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>San Martín governed with <b>Bernardo de Monteagudo</b> as his chief minister.</blockquote><a href="/state/protectorate-of-peru">Protectorate of Peru</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Tucumán Province, in what is now <a href="/countries/argentina">Argentina</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/peru">Peru</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-27,-65.5&amp;year=1812" aria-label="Tucumán Province on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2826 3154 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Bernardo de Monteagudo"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3276 3424h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3276 3424h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-27,-65.5&amp;year=1812">What was here in 1812? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
